# systems/ is the pure layer

Everything here is a framework-free function library. Game logic that can be expressed
without React should live here, not in a component or hook.

## Rules

- No imports from `react`, `@react-three/fiber`, `@react-three/rapier`, or
  `../stores/gameStore`. `three` is allowed, but only for math types
  (`Vector3`, `Quaternion`, `Euler`) — never scene objects, materials, or renderers.
- Export named functions with explicit return types. No default exports.
- Functions take their inputs as parameters and return new values. Do not mutate
  arguments; do not read or write module-level mutable state.
- Given the same inputs, a function returns the same output. Anything time-dependent
  takes the timestamp or delta as a parameter rather than calling `Date.now()`.
- Prefer a single object parameter once a function takes more than three arguments
  (see `detectShotType` in `shotTypes.ts`).
- Export the types alongside the functions that use them; `import type` for type-only imports.

## Domain accuracy

`scoring.ts` implements official WSF/PARS rules (PARS-11, win by 2 at 10–10, best of 3,
service-box alternation). When changing rule behaviour, cite the governing rule in a
comment the way the existing code does, and update the comment with the code.

Physics tuning in `shotContext.ts`, `hitAccuracy.ts`, and `swingAnimation.ts` is
deliberately unrealistic in places (light ball, oversized hit zone) for game feel. Treat
those constants as tuned values, not bugs.

## Style

No semicolons, single quotes, 2-space indent. Relative imports only — the `@/*` alias
has no Vite counterpart and will fail at runtime.
